// Medora — native enum types (RM M5.1).
//
// Every closed vocabulary in `DATABASE_SCHEMA_EXTRACTION.md` §6, as a
// PostgreSQL native ENUM. §14.3 fixes the choice: "native ENUM types for
// closed vocabularies". A varchar with a CHECK would accept a typo until
// someone read the constraint; an enum rejects it at parse time and gives
// the column a name in `pg_type` that migrations can alter deliberately.
//
// ── Why every member carries an explicit `@map` ───────────────────────
//
// The DB value is the API vocabulary. ADR-001 and ADR-002 are explicit
// that the literals are preserved verbatim, hyphens included — `no-show`,
// `awaiting-patient`, `pending-approval`, `critical-low`. Prisma
// identifiers cannot contain a hyphen, a space or a leading digit, so the
// member name and the stored value cannot always be the same string.
//
// They are therefore *never* assumed to be the same string. Every member
// maps explicitly, even where the two coincide (`active @map("active")`).
// The alternative — mapping only where the compiler forces it — leaves a
// reader working out which spelling reaches the database on a per-value
// basis, which is exactly the ambiguity the verbatim rule exists to stop.
//
// The consequence for the modules from M6 onward: the Prisma client speaks
// `NO_SHOW`, the wire speaks `no-show`, and the translation is a mapping
// the API layer owns. See ADR-023.
//
// ── Vocabularies that are deliberately NOT enums ──────────────────────
//
//  * `roles.code` — A1 allows administrators to create custom roles from a
//    base template (UX §3.9). A closed type cannot hold an open set.
//  * The §S1–S6 spec entities' status columns — DB §6 is the "complete
//    enum inventory" and does not list them; those columns are typed
//    `varchar` by their own specification and are closed by CHECK instead.
//  * `contact_role`, `presence`, `age_band`, `insight_kind` — listed in §6
//    but no column in §2 uses them; they are frontend view vocabularies.
//  * theme / density / focus_rings / date_format / time_format — §J1/§J2
//    type these as `varchar` with defaults, and are followed literally.

